UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53790642836754
DTSTART:20260830T200000Z
DTEND:20260830T213000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Beethoven’s iconic “Kreutzer” Sonata an
 d music of David Kirkland Garner\n\nThis season\, Gut Instinct takes on th
 e large-scale project “To Beethoven Rather Than To Seem\,” marking 200
  years since Beethoven’s death and 250 since the U.S. Declaration of Ind
 ependence. Over the course of five concerts\, Nicholas DiEugenio and piani
 st Mimi Solomon will present all ten Beethoven Sonatas on period instrumen
 ts along with five commissioned works written expressly for them.\n\n \n\n
 PARKING\n\nPlease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky.
  Learn more about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.\n\
 n \n\nThe Gut Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its third season\, i
 s a concert series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-Chapel Hill and D
 uke University’s historical instrument collections together with artists
  committed to presenting inspired\, visceral\, historically informed perfo
 rmances of the 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The series features 
 music of female composers and their artistic circles. The name “Gut Inst
 inct” is a play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the violin\, viola\, 
 and cello were strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the artists collabo
 rating with the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill (built in 19th-c
 entury Paris and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the Clementi piano at
  Duke (early 19th-century England) use these gut string materials. The tur
 n of phrase “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral\, accessible na
 ture of the musical performances that this series presents. Our mission is
  to make “Historically Informed Performance” accessible\, relatable\, 
 and culturally relevant! We also want to feature these special pianos that
  are rarely heard by the public\, along with great music by female compose
 rs which has remained lesser known to the greater public for far too long\
 , and living composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall\, Recital Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part I | Gut Instinct Chamb
 er Music Project
