DESCRIPTION:Zotero is a free\, easy-to-use tool to help you collect\, organ
 ize\, cite\, and share research. Attend this session to learn how to manag
 e your citations to make constructing your bibliography much easier. You
 ’ll be able to import citations from research databases like Academic Se
 arch Premier or Scopus\; use with word processing programs to insert citat
 ions into papers\; create a correctly formatted bibliography\; and use Gro
 ups to collaborate with other people. This class is structured for new to 
 beginning users.\n\n \n\nThis workshop is offered by the University Librar
 y and will be led by librarian Rolando Rodriguez and CALA Shannon Henry. \
 n\n \n\nThis workshop is being offered remotely via Zoom. Register here: h
 ttps://calendar.lib.unc.edu/event/17229826
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Creating Citations and Bibliographies with Zotero
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/creating-citations-and-bibliog
 raphies-with-zotero
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53753440037626
DTSTART:20260902T213000Z
DTEND:20260902T223000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join UNC Global Affairs for a Diplomatic Discussion with Jens-H
 inrich Binder\, professor and former dean of the faculty of law at the Uni
 versity of Tübingen. Binder will examine U.S.-EU relations through a lega
 l lens\, with particular attention to compliance law and transatlantic reg
 ulatory frameworks. His discussion will explore how legal structures shape
  cooperation — and friction — between the United States and the Europe
 an Union.\n\n \n\nWednesday\, Sept. 2\n5:30 p.m. (doors open at 5 p.m.)\nF
 edEx Global Education Center\, Nelson Mandela Auditorium \n\n \n\nJoin us 
 after the discussion for pizza in the atrium!\n\nRegistration form coming 
 soon to event webpage — CLE credit is available!\n\n \n\nThe Diplomatic 
 Discussion speaker series is part of Carolina’s Diplomacy Initiative\,
  which introduces students to shared global challenges and provides them o
 pportunities to practice skills necessary to address those challenges. In 
 2025\, UNC Global Affairs launched the Carolina Diplomacy Fellows for stud
 ents interested in deepening their engagement with the Diplomacy Initiativ
 e.  \n\n \n\nRead more about Jens-Hinrich Binder\n\nJens-Hinrich Binder 
 is professor of private law\, corporate law\, banking and securities law a
 t Eberhard Karls Universität Tübingen (since 2013)\, where he also serve
 s as co-director of the Tübingen Research Institute on Determinants of Ec
 onomic Activity (TRIDEA). He read law at the University of Freiburg from 1
 995\, from where he graduated in 2000 and obtained a doctorate in law in 2
 003. He also holds an LL.M. in banking and finance law from the London Sch
 ool of Economics and Political Science (2001). His undergraduate\, LL.M. a
 nd doctoral studies were funded by scholarships from Studienstiftung des D
 eutschen Volkes\, a non-political foundation\, and Bucerius Foundation\, r
 espectively. Having passed the Second State Examination (qualifying as a l
 egal practitioner) in 2004\, he completed his post-doctoral degree of Habi
 litation in 2010. Prior to joining the Tübingen Faculty\, he held a profe
 ssorship at EBS University (2011-13) and visiting professorial positions a
 t the Universities of Munich (2010) and Mainz (2010-11). \n\n \n\nHis rese
 arch interests focus on the areas of banking and securities regulation\, c
 orporate and insolvency law. He has published two treatises on bank insolv
 ency law and the regulation of corporations\, respectively\, as well as nu
 merous articles in both German and international edited journals and law r
 eviews. Both treatises have received awards for outstanding research from 
 the University of Freiburg. He serves on the editorial boards of two leadi
 ng German and one international banking and financial law journals\, as we
 ll as on an expert panel advising the German Federal Ministry of Finance o
 n matters of banking and financial regulation. As from 2025\, he also serv
 es as part-time judge in matters of corporate law at the Stuttgart Court o
 f Appeals. He has held visiting professorships at various universities in 
 Europe and Asia (including\, in 2023\, the Global Shimizu Visiting Profess
 orship at LSE Law School)\, lectures regularly within Germany and abroad\,
  and has been consulted by a wide range of German and European public and 
 governmental institutions. He is fellow academic member of the European Ba
 nking Institute (EBI) and research member of the European Corporate Instit
 ute (ecgi).
GEO:35.907828;-79.054177
LOCATION:FedEx Global Education Center\, Nelson Mandela Auditorium
SUMMARY:‘Sleepwalking into Estrangement’ with Jens-Hinrich Binder
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/sleepwalking-into-estrangement
 -with-jens-hinrich-binder
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
