UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53674317852993
DTSTART:20260903T213000Z
DTEND:20260903T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:Department of Art and Art History\n\n \n\nJes Fan’s interdisc
 iplinary practice explores the intersection of biology and identity\, inco
 rporating living matter\, as well as invisible substances like melanin and
  hormones\, that shape how we experience the world into their sculptures. 
 Through this process\, Fan looks at how these highly politicized materials
  form our understanding of the social constructs of race and gender\, and 
 the absurd pursuit of delimiting these as quantifiable amounts of material
 . Originally trained in glassmaking\, Fan combines hand-blown cellular gla
 ss forms with casts made from sections of human bodies\, in aqua resin and
  bearing uncanny flesh-like tones. Removed from the context of the figure\
 , these forms take on abstract qualities\, repeated and distorted across a
 rchitectural armatures\, suggesting an experience of the body increasingly
  intertwined with and mediated by technology. \n\n \n\nJes Fan lives and w
 orks in Brooklyn and Hong Kong. Fan's recent solo exhibition Unbounded was
  at Yale University Art Gallery\, New Haven\, in 2026. In 2024\, Andrew Kr
 eps Gallery presented Sites of Wounding: Interchapter\, Fan's first solo e
 xhibition in New York. The same year\, Fan was included in the 2024 Whitne
 y Biennial and Greater Art Toronto. In 2023\, Sites of Wounding: Chapter 2
  was on view at the M+ Museum in Hong Kong for the 2023 Sigg Prize exhibit
 ion. In 2022\, Fan participated in The Milk of Dreams\, the 59th Internati
 onal Art Exhibition of La Biennale di Venezia curated by Cecilia Alemani\,
  Venice. Fan’s work has been included in numerous group exhibitions: inc
 luding Scientia Sexualis\, at the Institute of Contemporary Art\, Los Ange
 les\, 2025\; Symbionts: Contemporary Artists and the Biosphere\, MIT List 
 Visual Arts Center\, 2022\; Breaking Water\, Contemporary Arts Center\, Ci
 ncinnati\, 2022\; Soft Water Hard Stone\, The Fifth New Museum Triennial\,
  New Museum\, New York\, 2021\; The Stomach and the Port\, Liverpool Bienn
 ale\, United Kingdom\, 2021\; NIRIN\, Biennale of Sydney\, Australia\, 202
 0\; The Socrates Annual 2019\, Socrates Sculpture Park\, Queens\, 2019. Fa
 n was awarded a Pollock-Krasner Grant in 2022.\n\n \n\nAn endowment establ
 ished in 1983 through the generosity of Nancy and Robin Hanes supports the
  Art Department's Visiting Artist Series. This important program brings bo
 th established and emerging artists to campus to discuss their work in pub
 lic lectures and to offer individual critiques to our M.F.A. students.  Th
 e Hanes Visiting Artist series greatly enriches both our academic programs
  and our outreach to the wider community.  All lectures are free and open 
 to the public.\n\n \n\nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after
  5:00 pm on weekdays. No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:
 30 am Monday. A $1.00 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More i
 nformation can be found HERE.\n\n \n\nContact: Martín Wannam\, mwanna@unc
 .edu\n\n \n\nImage Credit: Jes Fan\, Bivalve\, 2023\, polymer-modified gyp
 sum\, metal\, glass\, and pigment
GEO:35.91175;-79.048576
LOCATION:Hamilton Hall\, 100
SUMMARY:Hanes Visiting Artist Lecture Series: Jes Fan
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/hanes-visiting-artist-lecture-
 series-jes-fan
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53797256548461
DTSTART:20260903T233000Z
DTEND:20260904T003000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nDr. Juan Álamo\, vibraphone\nDr. Steve Anderson\,
  piano\nBrevan Hampden\, drum set\nAtiba Rorie\, percussion\nAndy Kleindie
 nst\, bass\n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nThis program celebrates John Coltrane’s ce
 ntennial with Latin jazz arrangements of his music\, reimagining his compo
 sitions through Afro-Caribbean\, Brazilian\, and Afro-Peruvian musical tra
 ditions while honoring the spirit of improvisation that defined his artist
 ry.\n\n \n\nADMISSION\n\nFree\, no reservation required.\n\n \n\nPARKING\n
 \nPlease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky. Learn mo
 re about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.
GEO:35.912607;-79.053261
LOCATION:Hill Hall\, Moeser Auditorium
SUMMARY:Viva Coltrane! – A Latin Jazz Centennial Tribute
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/viva-coltrane-a-latin-jazz-cen
 tennial-tribute
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53055342965871
DTSTART:20260904T120000Z
DTEND:20260904T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Art and Art History\n\nUnder the Tent: Whirling & Flickering\n\
 nAugust 20-October 15\, 2026\n\nReception: August 20\, 5-7 pm\n\n \n\nDraw
 n together in graduate school by their mutual love of experimentation and 
 play\, Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara Pearsall have built a collaborat
 ive practice around analogue animation and vibrant abstraction. Together\,
  their work explores optical toys\, immersive installations\, and movement
 . Under the Tent: Whirling & Flickering features kaleidoscopic imagery\, f
 lickering abstractions\, projection mapping\, a hidden trapdoor\, and hand
 -cranked wheels that transform what viewers see. After searching for the p
 erfect venue\, they are thrilled to bring their collaborative installation
  to UNC. Their first public artwork\, Turnabout Folly\, will debut in Lans
 ing\, Michigan in Fall 2026.\n\n \n\nMichelle Hinojosa works at the inters
 ection of painting\, poetry\, and fiber art\, where she considers memory\,
  intergenerational trauma\, and migration. Born in central Texas\, by way 
 of the fields of California and Northern Mexico\, Michelle uplifts traditi
 ons of making that have provided much-needed stability to a family displac
 ed. Michelle Hinojosa is an Assistant Professor of Art at Pennsylvania Sta
 te University.\n\n \n\nBarbara Pearsall is an artist and educator based in
  Michigan. Her art practice is attentive to how deviation from material co
 nventions can subvert or metamorphose the role of institutions\, where her
  philosophical and political concerns manifest through material exploratio
 ns of the built environment. Her pieces seek to create illusory and unusua
 l architectural conditions as a form of resistance\, interrupting real-wor
 ld functionality to allow for alternative systems of order. She is an Assi
 stant Professor of Foundations & Sculpture at Michigan State University.\n
 \n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Friday\, 8 am-5 pm\n\n \n\
 nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on weekdays. 
 No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday. A $1.00 
 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More information can be foun
 d HERE.\n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawrence\, 
 annettel@unc.edu
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara P
 earsall
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 michelle-inez-hinojosa-and-barbara-pearsall
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
