DESCRIPTION:When the thirteen colonies declared independence\, the new Unit
 ed States of America faced a central question: how could it create a stron
 g executive without a king? In honor of Constitution Day\, the Program for
  Public Discourse and the UNC School of Law will host a panel discussion o
 n the U.S. Constitution and executive power. Moderated by Suzanne Globetti
  (UNC)\, Michael Gerhardt (UNC) and Saikrishna Prakash (UVA) will discuss 
 the powers the Constitution granted the president and what they mean today
 .\n\n \n\nSeptember 17 is Constitution Day and Citizenship Day (Constituti
 on Day). This day commemorates the September 17\, 1787 signing of the U.S.
  Constitution.\n\n \n\nStudents who attend in person can earn CLE credit. 
 No tickets are necessary\; seating is first-come\, first-served. Reserved 
 tickets are available for guests traveling from outside Chapel Hill. Pleas
 e email publicdiscourse@unc.edu to reserve seats. We will serve pizza afte
 r the event.
GEO:35.907852;-79.050272
LOCATION:The Sonja Haynes Stone Center for Research in Black Culture and Hi
 story\, Multipurpose Room 
SUMMARY:Constitution Day: Panel Discussion on the Constitution and Executiv
 e Power 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/constitution-day-panel-discuss
 ion-on-the-constitution-and-executive-power
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
