UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53850130354338
DTSTART:20260914T213000Z
DTEND:20260914T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:Department of Art and Art History\n\n \n\n“Communities of Car
 e: Charles Alston\, Jacob Lawrence\, and the Murals of Harlem Hospital”\
 n\n \n\nWhen Jacob Lawrence first painted an image of Harlem Hospital in 1
 937\, Harlem’s largest public healthcare facility was teamed with artist
 s hired by the federal government and working under the supervision of Law
 rence’s art teacher and mentor\, Charles Alston. Although the young pain
 ter was not officially assigned to Alston’s team\, significant connectio
 ns to the murals can be found in his artwork\, in the murals themselves\, 
 and in their shared participation in a discourse on race\, medicine\, and 
 health in urban America. In exploring these connections\, this talk shows 
 how Black life could and could not be represented on the walls of Harlem H
 ospital\, and how a commitment to care within and for Black communities to
 ok shape in Lawrence’s art. \n\n \n\nTanya Sheehan is James M. Gillespie
  Professor and Chair of the Department of Science\, Technology\, and Socie
 ty at Colby College\; and Director of Academic and Scholarly Engagement at
  the Lunder Institute for American Art at the Colby College Museum of Art.
  She is the author of Doctored: The Medicine of Photography in Nineteenth-
 Century America (2011) and Study in Black and White: Photography\, Race\, 
 Humor (2018). Her edited books include Photography\, History\, Difference 
 (2014)\, Photography and Its Origins (2015\, with Andres Zervigon)\, Grove
  Art Guide to Photography (2017)\, Photography and Migration (2018)\, Andr
 ew Wyeth: Life and Death (2022)\, and Modernism\, Art\, Therapy (2024\, wi
 th Suzanne Hudson). She is currently a senior fellow at the Smithsonian Am
 erican Art Museum\, where she is completing a book that explores the subje
 cts of medicine and public health in art by African Americans.\n\n \n\nThi
 s lecture is co-sponsored by the University Library and HHIVE.\n\n \n\nA w
 eeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on weekdays. No p
 ermit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday. A $1.00 one-
 night pass is available in selected lots. More information can be found HE
 RE.\n\n \n\nContact: Maggie Cao\, mmcao@unc.edu
GEO:35.910043;-79.05257
LOCATION:Chapman Hall\, 125
SUMMARY:Lectures in Art History: Art\, Health\, and Equity series featuring
  Tanya Sheehan\, Colby College
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/lectures-in-art-history-art-he
 alth-and-equity-series-featuring-tanya-sheehan-colby-college
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53868294570514
DTSTART:20260914T220000Z
DTEND:20260915T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:UNC Hussman School of Journalism and Media alumnus Bryan Tu
 cker ’93 has written for Saturday Night Live for 20 years. During his ti
 me at SNL\, he’s had to find humor through four different presidents\, a
 n economic crisis\, a pandemic and two Duke basketball championships. It w
 asn’t always easy. \n\n \n\nIn this seminar\, Tucker takes students thro
 ugh some of the news events of the past two decades and gives insight on h
 ow SNL approached those events as comedy fodder for millions of viewers. T
 ucker will show clips and provide a firsthand look at the decisions and pr
 ocess of turning news into sketches.\n\n \n\nThis event is hosted by the U
 NC Hussman School of Journalism and Media and is open to the public. Learn
  more.
GEO:35.909113;-79.052767
LOCATION:Carroll Hall\, Knight Learning Lab (Carroll Hall 111)
SUMMARY:Turning headlines into humor with Bryan Tucker
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/turning-headlines-into-humor-w
 ith-bryan-tucker
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53379073586000
DTSTART:20260914T233000Z
DTEND:20260915T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:Yves Montand\, Jean-Louis Trintignant\, and Irène Papas headli
 ne this electrifying political thriller. A politician is assassinated in b
 road daylight\, setting in motion an investigation that gradually exposes 
 the distance between official narratives and lived reality. Racing ahead w
 ith remarkable urgency\, Z channels late-1960s anti-authoritarian currents
  and the growing sense that democratic institutions can no longer be taken
  at face value. \nThis film will be introduced by Professor Henry Veggian\
 , UNC-Chapel Hill Department of English and Comparative Literature. \nIt w
 ill be screened at 7:30 p.m. at The Lumina Theater (620 Market St\, Chapel
  Hill\, NC 27516). Tickets are free for all audience members\, and registr
 ation is required via The Lumina’s webpage. When this link is live\, cli
 ck to reserve your free ticket. \nUNC-Chapel Hill students can receive CLE
  credit for attending by scanning the QR code at the event. \n  \nAbout th
 e Series: \nJoin the Ackland Film Forum this fall for Socially Charged Cri
 me Thrillers: Snapshots of Social Anxiety\, a series that accompanies the 
 Ackland exhibition Care and Concern: Recent Acquisitions of Modern and Con
 temporary Art. \nAs vehicles of social commentary and stylistic bravura\, 
 crime thrillers often register the pressures and contradictions of their h
 istorical moment. Across nearly half a century\, the films in this series 
 stage encounters with political power\, public trust\, inequality\, and am
 bition through assassination plots\, botched robberies\, and media-driven 
 spectacle. \nFrom the crises of the late 1960s to the intensified media en
 vironments of the twenty-first century\, these works show how genre cinema
  can capture social anxiety in real time. Each film stands both as a gripp
 ing narrative and as a record of a world under strain\, where institutions
  falter and everyday life becomes fraught with conflict. \nThe Ackland Fil
 m Forum is co-organized by the Ackland Art Museum and UNC Film Studies\, h
 oused in the UNC-Chapel Hill Department of English and Comparative Literat
 ure.
LOCATION:The Lumina Theater\, 620 Market Street\, Chapel Hill\, 27516\, Uni
 ted States
SUMMARY:Ackland Film Forum: "Z" (Costa-Gavras\, 1969)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ackland-film-forum-z-costa-gav
 ras-1969
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041912Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53055342973046
DTSTART:20260915T120000Z
DTEND:20260915T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Art and Art History\n\nUnder the Tent: Whirling & Flickering\n\
 nAugust 20-October 15\, 2026\n\nReception: August 20\, 5-7 pm\n\n \n\nDraw
 n together in graduate school by their mutual love of experimentation and 
 play\, Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara Pearsall have built a collaborat
 ive practice around analogue animation and vibrant abstraction. Together\,
  their work explores optical toys\, immersive installations\, and movement
 . Under the Tent: Whirling & Flickering features kaleidoscopic imagery\, f
 lickering abstractions\, projection mapping\, a hidden trapdoor\, and hand
 -cranked wheels that transform what viewers see. After searching for the p
 erfect venue\, they are thrilled to bring their collaborative installation
  to UNC. Their first public artwork\, Turnabout Folly\, will debut in Lans
 ing\, Michigan in Fall 2026.\n\n \n\nMichelle Hinojosa works at the inters
 ection of painting\, poetry\, and fiber art\, where she considers memory\,
  intergenerational trauma\, and migration. Born in central Texas\, by way 
 of the fields of California and Northern Mexico\, Michelle uplifts traditi
 ons of making that have provided much-needed stability to a family displac
 ed. Michelle Hinojosa is an Assistant Professor of Art at Pennsylvania Sta
 te University.\n\n \n\nBarbara Pearsall is an artist and educator based in
  Michigan. Her art practice is attentive to how deviation from material co
 nventions can subvert or metamorphose the role of institutions\, where her
  philosophical and political concerns manifest through material exploratio
 ns of the built environment. Her pieces seek to create illusory and unusua
 l architectural conditions as a form of resistance\, interrupting real-wor
 ld functionality to allow for alternative systems of order. She is an Assi
 stant Professor of Foundations & Sculpture at Michigan State University.\n
 \n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Friday\, 8 am-5 pm\n\n \n\
 nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on weekdays. 
 No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday. A $1.00 
 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More information can be foun
 d HERE.\n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawrence\, 
 annettel@unc.edu
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara P
 earsall
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 michelle-inez-hinojosa-and-barbara-pearsall
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
