DTSTAMP:20260905T041913Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53803881172701
DTSTART:20260917T213000Z
DTEND:20260917T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:What do a Civil War-era plantation owner and a modern “girl b
 oss” have in common? More than you think. \nAdelicia Acklen and Dorothy 
 Thomas were two of the most powerful plantation-owning women—one in the 
 US South and the other in South America—but both wielded the power of ow
 nership by deploying and playing on particular ideas of femininity. This t
 alk hosted by Carolina Public Humanities traces their unsettling legacy in
 to the present\, asking what happens when women gain power within systems 
 built on inequality. \nFree\, but space is limited\; registration is requi
 red via the Carolina Public Humanities event webpage. \nAbout the speaker:
  \nTanya Shields is an Associate Professor of Women’s and Gender Studies
  whose research focuses on Caribbean studies and plantation logics. She te
 aches courses on the Caribbean\, the arts of activism\, growing up girls g
 lobally\, and the continuing influence of plantation economics and politic
 s. She is currently working on her second monograph\, Gendered Labor: Race
 \, Place\, and Power on Female-Owned Plantations\, a comparative study of 
 women who owned plantations in the Caribbean and the U.S. South.
GEO:35.912593;-79.054867
LOCATION:Ackland Art Museum
SUMMARY:“She Will Be Quite a Manager”: From Plantation Owner to Girl Bo
 ss
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/she-will-be-quite-a-manager-fr
 om-plantation-owner-to-girl-boss
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
