DTSTART:20260923T210000Z
DTEND:20260923T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:North Carolina’s 1776 Constitution grew out of two crises: an
  east-west power struggle that erupted into the War of the Regulation\, an
 d a fight for political representation against an increasingly hostile Eng
 lish government. This lecture traces that story back to the religious and 
 political conflicts of 17th-century Britain\, through the migrations and e
 conomic pressures that shaped colonial North Carolina\, to the document th
 at remains the state’s constitutional foundation today. \n\n \n\nThe lec
 ture is presented by an interdisciplinary team from Carolina Law\, the His
 tory Department and the University Libraries.
GEO:35.90984;-79.050769
LOCATION:Wilson Library \, Pleasants Assembly Room
SUMMARY:The 1776 North Carolina Constitution
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-1776-north-carolina-consti
 tution
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041913Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53868328021634
DTSTART:20260923T213000Z
DTEND:20260923T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:“Access\, Activism\, and Aesthetic Revision: Crip Methods for
  Art History”\n\n \n\nThis talk brings together the core arguments of Th
 e Agency of Access: Contemporary Disability Art and Institutional Critique
  (2024)\, Hospital Aesthetics: Disability\, Medicine\, Activism (2025)\, a
 nd Rehabilitating the Asylum: Mental Health Justice and Contemporary Art (
 2027) to demonstrate that disability is not peripheral to canonical art\; 
 instead\, it is one of its most generative methodological engines to arise
  with the canon of 20th century art history. Across these three books\, Am
 anda Cachia develops what she calls a critical-aesthetic approach to acces
 s\, care\, and confinement\, tracing how hospitals\, asylums\, and contemp
 orary art institutions function as overlapping architectures of power\, cr
 eativity\, and resistance. Moving between art history\, disability studies
 \, medical humanities\, institutional critique\, and the activist strategi
 es of contemporary disability artists\, Cachia shows how access operates a
 s an artistic medium and how care becomes an aesthetic category. By center
 ing disability as both method and material\, she proposes an art history t
 hat is structurally transformed by the politics and poetics of access\, an
 d how pertinent historical artworks and movements can be reconfigured base
 d on a crip revision. \n\n \n\nAmanda Cachia is Professor of Practice in M
 useum Studies in the School of Art at the Herberger Institute for Design a
 nd the Arts at Arizona State University and Affiliate Faculty in the Disab
 ility Studies B.A. Her research interests include disability art history\,
  theory and activism\, crip curatorial practices and access aesthetics\, m
 useums\, institutional critique\, and social justice\, and critical disabi
 lity approaches to translation\, movement\, medicine\, and health. Cachia 
 is the author of three books\, Rehabilitating the Asylum: Mental Health Ju
 stice and Contemporary Art\, (forthcoming 2027)\, Hospital Aesthetics: Dis
 ability\, Medicine\, Activism (2025)\, and The Agency of Access: Contempor
 ary Disability Art and Institutional Critique (2024)\, the latter of which
  was shortlisted for the College Art Association’s 2026 Charles Rufus Mo
 rey Book Award. She is also editor of Curating Access: Disability Art Acti
 vism and Creative Accommodation (2022)\, which includes over 40 internatio
 nal contributors. She is currently working on her fourth book project\, Di
 sability Art: A Political History. Since 2010\, Cachia has organized 20 ar
 t exhibitions devoted to the work of disabled artists. She is currently de
 veloping the major touring exhibition Vital Signs: From Patient to Power w
 ith Mid-America Arts Alliance and ExhibitsUSA\, which will be hosted by nu
 merous galleries across the United States from 2027-2032. Cachia is also a
  curatorial and disability advisor for a major exhibition being developed 
 about art and disability from the 19th century to the present at the Los A
 ngeles County Museum of Art in 2028.\n\n \n\nA weeknight or daytime permit
  is now required after 5 p.m. on weekdays. No permit is required from 5 p.
 m. Friday through 7:30 a.m. Monday. A $1 one-night pass is available in se
 lected lots. More information can be found HERE.\n\n \n\nContact: Maggie C
 ao\, mmcao@unc.edu
GEO:35.910043;-79.05257
LOCATION:Chapman Hall\, 125
SUMMARY:Lectures in Art History: Art\, Health\, Equity Series featuring Ama
 nda Cachia\, Arizona State University
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/lectures-in-art-history-art-he
 alth-equity-series-featuring-amanda-cachia-arizona-state-university
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
