DESCRIPTION:Dr. Dave Fossum\, Arizona State University\, National Humanitie
 s Center \n“What’s an Arrangement Worth?: Copyright\, Folk Music\, and
  the Value of Creativity in Turkey”\nCopyright is supposed to reward cre
 ators for their creativity. Yet there are no clear criteria for measuring 
 the value of this creativity or how creative a work is. Drawing on a case 
 study from my book\, Copyright Consciousness\, this talk links implicit cr
 iteria of creative value to a larger history of nation-building. The launc
 hing point for my inquiry is a policy of Turkey’s copyright collecting s
 ocieties\, who long offered arrangers of public domain folk music only a (
 10%) royalty on their arrangements (whereas a 100% royalty is common in mo
 st jurisdictions). While some arrangers complained about this policy\, my 
 ethnographic data reveal that many musicians found it natural. Through ana
 lyses of a range of arrangements of folk tunes and songs\, I highlight the
  forms of creative agency often erased by this widespread tendency to mini
 mize their originality. Several factors might account for this tendency\, 
 including industry politics\, bureaucratic convenience\, and legal doctrin
 e\, yet none explains the policy as well as folkloristic ideologies that l
 ocate creative agency primarily in a romanticized vision of the nation’s
  past. The stakes of this finding transcend a normative critique of the po
 licy itself. Rather\, my analysis highlights how Turkey’s history of nat
 ionalist cultural reformism gets reinscribed into copyright\, despite its 
 ostensibly identity-blind framing. This finding in turn illustrates a larg
 er claim at the core of my book: that the construction of copyright legali
 ty is an emergent and contingent process shaped by a wide range of actors\
 , from courts to composers.
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:Dr. Dave Fossum | UNC Music Research Forum
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/dr-dave-fossum-unc-music-resea
 rch-forum
CATEGORIES:Meetings and Conferences
END:VEVENT
