DTSTAMP:20260905T041913Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53806417702155
DTSTART:20260929T210000Z
DTEND:20260929T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:North Carolina’s 1868 Constitution rebuilt the state after th
 e Civil War\, expanding political participation and redefining the relatio
 nship between government and citizens in the state’s first attempt at mu
 ltiracial democracy. This lecture examines how that document laid the grou
 ndwork for principles that continue to shape North Carolina constitutional
  law\, and what its lessons offer for constitutional debates still unresol
 ved 250 years after the state’s founding. \n\n \n\nSpeakers: Marcus Gads
 on\, associate professor of law\, and Harry L. Watson\, Atlanta Distinguis
 hed Professor of Southern Culture \n\n \n\nAbout the Speakers:\n\n \n\nPro
 fessor Marcus Gadson is an associate professor of law at UNC School of Law
 \, joining the faculty in 2025. He teaches courses on State Constitutional
  Law\, federal Constitutional Law\, and civil procedure. He is one of the 
 nation’s leading authorities on state constitutions and has published a 
 book arguing that constitutional crises at the state level have profoundly
  shaped American constitutional law and co-authored a casebook about state
  constitutions. Before joining Carolina\, Professor Gadson taught at Campb
 ell University School of Law and practiced at Steptoe & Johnson in Washing
 ton\, D.C. \n\n \n\nProfessor Harry L. Watson is the Atlanta Distinguished
  Professor of Southern Culture at UNC-Chapel Hill. A native of Greensboro\
 , he researches the political\, social and intellectual history of the ant
 ebellum South and Jacksonian America\, including the formation of politica
 l parties and the relationship between race and class under slavery. He di
 rected the UNC Center for the Study of the American South from 1999 to 201
 2 and edited its journal\, Southern Cultures\, from 1993 to 2019. He is th
 e author or editor of eight books\, including Liberty and Power: The Polit
 ics of Jacksonian America and Building the American Republic\, Volume 1: A
  Narrative History to 1877. Watson holds a bachelor's from Brown Universit
 y and a doctorate from Northwestern.
GEO:35.90984;-79.050769
LOCATION:Wilson Library \, Pleasants Assembly Room
SUMMARY:The 1868 North Carolina Constitution
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-1868-north-carolina-consti
 tution
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041913Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53825991864616
DTSTART:20260929T213000Z
DTEND:20260929T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:The Abbey Speaker Series returns with a conversation about one 
 of the most important issues in American life. How should the United State
 s enforce immigration laws? What should the future of legal immigration lo
 ok like? When should refugees be granted asylum?\n\n \n\nJoin us to hear f
 rom three experts who offer different perspectives on immigration: Jim Rob
 b (NumbersUSA)\, Diego Sánchez (The Presidents’ Alliance on Higher Educ
 ation and Immigration)\, and Peter Skerry (Boston College). Daniel DiSalvo
 \, associate dean of the School of Civic Life and Leadership\, will modera
 te the conversation.\n\n \n\nStudents who attend in person can earn CLE cr
 edit. No tickets are necessary\; seating is first-come\, first-served. Res
 erved tickets are available for guests traveling from outside Chapel Hill.
  Please email publicdiscourse@unc.edu to reserve seats. Join us for pizza 
 in the lobby afterward!
GEO:35.909991;-79.047621
LOCATION:The Frank Porter Graham Student Union\, Auditorium
SUMMARY:Immigration: A Conversation Across Perspectives 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/immigration-a-conversation-acr
 oss-perspectives
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
