DESCRIPTION:How can the way we listen change when music is presented as par
 t of a continuing story\, rather than one moment in time? Does it make us 
 feel more open\, flexible\, or curious?\n\n \n\nBeing in the audience for 
 Shifting Ground might feel a bit like standing on the bank of a flowing ri
 ver: being able to look toward what is coming or to turn and see what has 
 passed\, all from one vantage point.\n\n \n\nViolinist Alexi Kenney invite
 s audiences into this time-traveling musical journey which includes works 
 by J.S. Bach interwoven with those of contemporary creators\, including Ra
 fiq Bhatia\, Kaija Saariaho\, Paul Wiancko\, Salina Fisher and Angélica N
 egrón—all accompanied by projection art by Xuan. Along the way\, audien
 ces encounter deeply affecting moments including a work that pairs solo vi
 olin with the recorded sounds of a melting glacier. \n\n \n\nAs music and 
 imagery unfold together\, familiar sounds take on new meaning and unexpect
 ed connections emerge. The result is an experience of music as a living co
 nversation across generations that rewards deep listening.\n\n \n\nStudent
  tickets always $11. Eligible for CLE. Find out more and buy tickets at: h
 ttps://carolinaperformingarts.org/events/shiftingground/
LOCATION:Hill Hall\, Moeser Auditorium
SUMMARY:Alexi Kenney\, violin Shifting Ground Projection art by Xuan
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/alexi-kenney-violin-shifting-g
 round-projection-art-by-xuan
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
