DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Allen Anderson’s “Linen\,” plus Beethov
 en Violin Sonatas Nos. 6 and 7\n\nThis season\, Gut Instinct takes on the 
 large-scale project “To Beethoven Rather Than To Seem\,” marking 200 y
 ears since Beethoven’s death and 250 since the U.S. Declaration of Indep
 endence. Over the course of five concerts\, Nicholas DiEugenio and pianist
  Mimi Solomon will present all ten Beethoven Sonatas on period instruments
  along with five commissioned works written expressly for them.\n\n \n\nPA
 RKING\n\nPlease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky. L
 earn more about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.\n\n 
 \n\nThe Gut Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its third season\, is 
 a concert series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-Chapel Hill and Duk
 e University’s historical instrument collections together with artists c
 ommitted to presenting inspired\, visceral\, historically informed perform
 ances of the 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The series features mu
 sic of female composers and their artistic circles. The name “Gut Instin
 ct” is a play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the violin\, viola\, an
 d cello were strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the artists collabora
 ting with the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill (built in 19th-cen
 tury Paris and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the Clementi piano at D
 uke (early 19th-century England) use these gut string materials. The turn 
 of phrase “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral\, accessible natu
 re of the musical performances that this series presents. Our mission is t
 o make “Historically Informed Performance” accessible\, relatable\, an
 d culturally relevant! We also want to feature these special pianos that a
 re rarely heard by the public\, along with great music by female composers
  which has remained lesser known to the greater public for far too long\, 
 and living composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part II | Gut Instinct Cham
 ber Music Project
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-se
 em-part-ii-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041914Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53682637377333
DTSTART:20261108T000000Z
DTEND:20261108T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:Some performances entertain. Others leave audiences seeing the 
 world differently. In EcoTones\, music\, storytelling\, research\, persona
 l history\, and lived experience come together in an experience that feels
  less like a performance and more like a gathering. Warm\, joyful\, and ch
 allenging\, the work invites audiences to consider what happens when diffe
 rent worlds meet and what becomes possible in the spaces between them.\n\n
  \n\nInspired by the ecological concept of an ecotone—the place where di
 stinct ecosystems overlap and create the conditions for new life—EcoTone
 s explores the intersections that shape our lives\, communities\, and futu
 res. The result is a shared act of discovery that reveals unexpected conne
 ctions and invites audiences to see familiar questions from new perspectiv
 es.\n\n \n\nCreated by Toshi Reagon in collaboration with UNC-Chapel Hill 
 scholars Danielle Spurlock and Danielle Purifoy and Durham-based writer\, 
 scholar\, and activist Alexis Pauline Gumbs\, the performance places artis
 ts and researchers side by side on stage. Their stories\, questions\, idea
 s\, and experiences become part of the work itself\, reflecting Toshi’s 
 belief that important voices and perspectives in our communities belong in
  shared public conversation.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. Eligible 
 for CLE. Find out more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperformingarts.
 org/events/toshireagon-ecotones/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:Toshi Reagon EcoTones
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/toshi-reagon-ecotones
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
