DTSTART:20270228T220000Z
DTEND:20270228T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Beethoven’s final Violin Sonata\, the elegi
 ac and enigmatic op. 96\, along with Tonia Ko’s Tribute: Axis II\n\nThis
  season\, Gut Instinct takes on the large-scale project “To Beethoven Ra
 ther Than To Seem\,” marking 200 years since Beethoven’s death and 250
  since the U.S. Declaration of Independence. Over the course of five conce
 rts\, Nicholas DiEugenio and pianist Mimi Solomon will present all ten Bee
 thoven Sonatas on period instruments along with five commissioned works wr
 itten expressly for them.\n\nThis concert is presented as part of the NCHI
 P Festival.\n\n \n\nADMISSION\n\nFree\, no reservation required.\n\n \n\nP
 ARKING\n\nPlease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky. 
 Learn more about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.\n\n
  \n\nThe Gut Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its third season\, is
  a concert series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-Chapel Hill and Du
 ke University’s historical instrument collections together with artists 
 committed to presenting inspired\, visceral\, historically informed perfor
 mances of the 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The series features m
 usic of female composers and their artistic circles. The name “Gut Insti
 nct” is a play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the violin\, viola\, a
 nd cello were strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the artists collabor
 ating with the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill (built in 19th-ce
 ntury Paris and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the Clementi piano at 
 Duke (early 19th-century England) use these gut string materials. The turn
  of phrase “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral\, accessible nat
 ure of the musical performances that this series presents. Our mission is 
 to make “Historically Informed Performance” accessible\, relatable\, a
 nd culturally relevant! We also want to feature these special pianos that 
 are rarely heard by the public\, along with great music by female composer
 s which has remained lesser known to the greater public for far too long\,
  and living composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part IV | Gut Instinct Cham
 ber Music Project
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-se
 em-part-iv-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
