DESCRIPTION:For anyone who has ever questioned their place\, pushed against
  expectations\, or wondered what it costs to remain true to yourself\, The
  Surge: An Ode to Sinéad O’Connor is for you.\n\nInspired by the music\
 , words\, and enduring spirit of Sinéad O’Connor\, director and choreog
 rapher Sonya Tayeh creates a powerful dance experience performed by ten wo
 men over the age of 40. O’Connor’s music and narration\, drawn from he
 r memoir Rememberings\, weave throughout the performance. \n\n \n\nThe Sur
 ge is a reflection on identity\, aging\, freedom\, and the courage require
 d to live authentically in a world that often rewards conformity. What eme
 rges is both deeply personal and unexpectedly communal: a celebration of w
 omen who refuse to disappear\, soften themselves for others\, or surrender
  the right to define their own lives.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. 
 Eligible for CLE. Find out more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperfor
 mingarts.org/events/the-surge/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:The Surge: An Ode to Sinéad O’Connor
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-surge-an-ode-to-sinead-oco
 nnor
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041914Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53877068117645
DTSTART:20270319T193000Z
DTEND:20270319T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Dr. Eric Debrah-Otchere\, University of Cape Coast\, National H
 umanities Center\n\n“Title TBA”\n\n \n\nEric Debrah Otchere teaches in
  the Department of Music and Dance (Faculty of Arts\, College of Humanitie
 s and Legal Studies) at the University of Cape Coast\, Ghana. He specializ
 es in the field(s) of Music Education/Music Psychology and handles various
  courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. He is the recipien
 t of several prestigious awards/fellowships such as a Presidential Fellow 
 of the African Studies Association (USA)\, AHP-ACLS/Rhodes University Post
 -Doctoral Fellow\, and a Stellenbosch Institute for Advanced Studies (STIA
 S) Iso Lomso Fellow. His research interests cover areas in Music Education
  (e.g. pedagogy\, philosophy\, curriculum\, research methods) and Music Ps
 ychology (e.g. music and emotions\, musical development\, music preference
 s\, music cognition\, emotional intelligence\, health and wellbeing). He h
 as a number of publications in International peer-reviewed journals\, some
  of which he has also reviewed papers for.\n\n \n\nThe UNC Music Research 
 Forum hosts invited lectures by distinguished music scholars from around t
 he world and informal research presentations by members of our department 
 and colleagues across campus. It serves as an intellectual hub for the dep
 artment and is open to all. The Forum is usually held on the final Friday 
 of each month during the semester from 3:30-5:00 pm in Person Recital Hall
 .\n\n \n\nAdmission: Free\, No Reservation Required
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall\, Recital Hall
SUMMARY:Dr. Eric Debrah-Otchere | UNC Music Research Forum
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/dr-eric-debrah-otchere-unc-mus
 ic-research-forum
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041914Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53682694635227
DTSTART:20270319T230000Z
DTEND:20270320T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Some performances—especially jazz—invite you to surrender t
 he need to know what’s coming next. You stop trying to predict the next 
 note and simply hold your breath\, waiting to discover where the music wil
 l lead. That uncertainty is the thrill: performers and audience alike foll
 owing the sound somewhere unexpected\, until you find yourself wondering\,
  How did they do that?\n\n \n\nThe Vijay Iyer Quartet lives in that space 
 of possibility. Performing compositions by composer-pianist Vijay Iyer\, t
 he ensemble reveals new possibilities in the music each time it takes the 
 stage.\n\n \n\nVoted DownBeat‘s Jazz Artist of the Year four times and a
  MacArthur Fellow\, Iyer has spent decades expanding the possibilities of 
 the jazz quartet. His celebrated trio with bassist Linda May Han Oh and dr
 ummer Tyshawn Sorey was praised by The New York Times as “one of the bes
 t bands in jazz.”\n\n \n\nBuilding on three decades of musical collabora
 tion and experimentation\, Iyer expanded the ensemble in 2025 with the add
 ition of trumpeter Adam O’Farrill\, a longtime collaborator and member o
 f one of Afro-Latin music’s most revered musical families.\n\n \n\nToget
 her\, the quartet combines acoustic instruments with Fender Rhodes and ele
 ctronics\, creating music that is fearless\, unpredictable\, and constantl
 y unfolding.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. Eligible for CLE. Find ou
 t more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperformingarts.org/events/thevi
 jayiyerquartet/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:The Vijay Iyer Quartet
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-vijay-iyer-quartet
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
