DTSTART:20270501T233000Z
DTEND:20270502T003000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Beethoven’s “Spring” Sonata and Robert 
 Honstein’s Olmsted\n\nThis season\, Gut Instinct takes on the large-scal
 e project “To Beethoven Rather Than To Seem\,” marking 200 years since
  Beethoven’s death and 250 since the U.S. Declaration of Independence. O
 ver the course of five concerts\, Nicholas DiEugenio and pianist Mimi Solo
 mon will present all ten Beethoven Sonatas on period instruments along wit
 h five commissioned works written expressly for them.\n\n \n\nPARKING\n\nP
 lease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky. Learn more 
 about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.\n\n \n\nThe Gu
 t Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its third season\, is a concert 
 series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-Chapel Hill and Duke Universi
 ty’s historical instrument collections together with artists committed t
 o presenting inspired\, visceral\, historically informed performances of t
 he 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The series features music of fem
 ale composers and their artistic circles. The name “Gut Instinct” is a
  play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the violin\, viola\, and cello we
 re strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the artists collaborating with 
 the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill (built in 19th-century Paris
  and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the Clementi piano at Duke (early
  19th-century England) use these gut string materials. The turn of phrase 
 “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral\, accessible nature of the 
 musical performances that this series presents. Our mission is to make “
 Historically Informed Performance” accessible\, relatable\, and cultural
 ly relevant! We also want to feature these special pianos that are rarely 
 heard by the public\, along with great music by female composers which has
  remained lesser known to the greater public for far too long\, and living
  composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part V | Gut Instinct Chamb
 er Music Project
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-se
 em-part-v-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
